    ],
    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-022-00576-6",
    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Aerosol-climate interactions",
    "problem": "Global climate models struggle to consistently simulate historical precipitation change because aerosol-induced shortwave absorption is poorly constrained by observations and varies widely between models, creating a dominant and irreducible source of uncertainty.",
    "method": "The paper proposes a method to quantify precipitation change uncertainty by applying an emergent linear relationship between aerosol absorption and precipitation inhibition, which is derived by synthesizing results from multiple climate model intercomparison projects.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify how the large uncertainty in simulated aerosol absorption in CMIP6 climate models is a dominating cause of the spread in simulated historical precipitation change, both globally and regionally.",
